              "segment": {
                "start_index": null,
                "end_index": 190,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "When people refer to \"clean,\" \"pure,\" or \"fine\" gold, they are speaking of 24-karat (24K) gold, which is stamped with \"999\" (indicating **99.9% purity**) or sometimes \"999.9\" (99.99% purity)"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                2,
                3
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 194,
                "end_index": 336,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "While it might seem logical to expect \"pure\" gold to be 100% pure, achieving absolute 100% purity is scientifically and practically impossible"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                4
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 544,
                "end_index": 622,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "A single gram of gold contains roughly $3 \\times 10^{21}$ (3 sextillion) atoms"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                5,
                4,
                6
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 624,
                "end_index": 867,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "According to the laws of physics and thermodynamics (specifically entropy), it is statistically impossible to arrange sextillions of atoms so perfectly that *not a single one* of them is a foreign element (like silver, copper, iron, or carbon)"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                7
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 943,
                "end_index": 1120,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "Limits of Chemical Refining\nGold is purified using industrial refining methods, most notably:\n*   **The Miller Process:** This involves bubbling chlorine gas through molten gold"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                8
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 1122,
                "end_index": 1230,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "The chlorine binds to impurities (like copper and silver), turning them into chlorides that float to the top"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                7
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 1232,
                "end_index": 1304,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "This process is highly efficient but maxes out at about **99.5% purity**"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                7
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 1306,
                "end_index": 1395,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "*   **The Wohlwill Process:** To get gold cleaner, refiners use this electrolytic process"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                9,
                7,
                10
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 1397,
                "end_index": 1480,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "Impure gold is dissolved in an acid bath and electroplated onto a pure gold cathode"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                7
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 1482,
                "end_index": 1701,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "While it is incredibly precise, microscopic trace amounts of other metals with similar chemical properties (like silver or platinum-group metals) will still migrate with the gold, leaving a threshold of about **99.99%**"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                9
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 1712,
                "end_index": 1909,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "Immediate Re-contamination\nEven if a laboratory somehow managed to isolate 100% pure gold in a perfect vacuum, the moment that gold is handled, melted, or cast into a bar, it would lose that status"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                9
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 1912,
                "end_index": 2001,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "When molten gold is poured, it touches crucibles, graphite molds, and the surrounding air"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                5
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 2003,
                "end_index": 2099,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "It inevitably absorbs microscopic trace amounts of carbon, oxygen, dust, or metal from the tools"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                5
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 2110,
                "end_index": 2252,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "Limits of Measurement (Assaying)\nTo legally declare something \"100% pure,\" you must have a way to prove there is absolutely nothing else in it"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                5,
                8
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 2255,
                "end_index": 2433,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "Modern testing methods\u2014such as *fire assaying* or *inductively coupled plasma mass spectrometry (ICP-MS)*\u2014are incredibly precise, but they still have a **limit of detection**"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                5
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 2435,
                "end_index": 2569,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "Because no machine can detect with 100.0000% certainty that zero impurity atoms exist, mints and refiners must leave a margin of error"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                5
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 2571,
                "end_index": 2649,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "Stating \"99.9%\" or \"99.99%\" is a legally and scientifically accurate guarantee"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                11
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 2660,
                "end_index": 2761,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "Diminishing Returns (Exponential Cost)\nIn refining, the \"Rule of Diminishing Returns\" applies heavily"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                12
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 2763,
                "end_index": 2808,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "Removing 90% of impurities is relatively easy"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                12
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 2810,
                "end_index": 2832,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "Removing 99% is harder"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                7,
                13
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 2834,
                "end_index": 2926,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "Getting from 99.9% to 99.99% requires massive amounts of energy, specialized acids, and time"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                14,
                13
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 2929,
                "end_index": 3134,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "Chasing the absolute 100% mark would cost millions of dollars for a fraction of a gram, resulting in a product that is far too expensive to sell and offers no functional difference to investors or jewelers"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                2
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 3143,
                "end_index": 3322,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "### Fun Fact: The Cleanest Gold Ever Made\nIn 1957, the Perth Mint in Australia produced a plate of gold that reached **999.999 fineness** (99.9999% pure), often called \"six nines\""
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                7
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 3324,
                "end_index": 3477,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "It is widely considered the highest purity of gold ever achieved, but even this legendary achievement was still 0.0001% short of being absolute 100% gold"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            }
          ],
